  "dqlnbVersion": 1,
  "version": 1,
  "title": "Jaffle Shop — Welcome",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Jaffle Shop — Welcome",
    "savedWith": "datalex-cloud"
  },
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "intro",
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "# Jaffle Shop + DQL\n\nThis workspace adds a DQL governed-analytics layer on top of the standard Jaffle Shop dbt project. The connection points at the dbt-built `jaffle_shop.duckdb`, the dbt DAG is synced, and there are **10 certified blocks** plus a **Jaffle Analytics** App with an executive dashboard.\n\nRun the cells below, open **Blocks**, **Apps**, and **Lineage** in the left rail, then follow [`TUTORIAL.md`](../TUTORIAL.md) for a guided tour and to build your own block."
    },
    {
      "id": "revenue_raw",
      "type": "sql",
      "content": "SELECT date_trunc('month', ordered_at) AS month, SUM(order_total) AS revenue\nFROM dev.orders\nGROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1",
      "name": "Revenue by month (raw query)"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-4-1781199838092",
      "type": "dql",
      "content": "// dql-format: 1\n\nblock \"top_customers\" {\n  domain      = \"customers\"\n  type        = \"custom\"\n  status      = \"certified\"\n  owner       = \"analytics@jaffle.shop\"\n  description = \"Top 10 customers by lifetime spend, with order counts.\"\n  tags        = [\"customers\", \"ranking\", \"loyalty\"]\n  llmContext  = \"Highest-value customers: orders the dbt customers mart by lifetime_spend descending, top 10, showing customer_name, lifetime_spend and count_lifetime_orders. Use for 'best customers' / 'who spends the most' questions.\"\n  examples = [{ question = \"Who are our most valuable customers?\" }]\n  query = \"\"\"\n    SELECT customer_name, lifetime_spend, count_lifetime_orders\n    FROM dev.customers\n    ORDER BY lifetime_spend DESC\n    LIMIT 10\n  \"\"\"\n  visualization {\n    chart = \"table\"\n  }\n  tests {\n    assert row_count >= 1\n  }\n}\n"
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-3-1781199835874",
      "type": "chart",
      "content": "-- Replace with your own table. This example generates sample data.\nSELECT unnest(['Q1','Q2','Q3','Q4']) AS quarter,\n       unnest([42500, 58300, 61200, 74100]) AS revenue"
    },
    {
      "id": "customer_mix",
      "type": "sql",
      "content": "SELECT customer_type, COUNT(*) AS customers, SUM(lifetime_spend) AS lifetime_spend\nFROM dev.customers\nGROUP BY 1 ORDER BY lifetime_spend DESC",
      "name": "Customer mix"
    }
  ]
}
